# ************************************
#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 0.1.10b
# ************************************
# DESCRIPTION
# Determine the domain and range of f(x) = sqrt(A^2 - x^2)

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject('Calculus')
## DBchapter('Before Calculus')
## DBsection('Functions')
## KEYWORDS('Functions', 'Domain', 'Range')
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## EditionText1('9')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## Section1('0.1')
## Problem1('10b')
## Author('')
## Institution('John Wiley & Sons')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");
#****************************************************
#  Create function of form sqrt(A^2 - x^2)
#****************************************************
$A    = random(1, 10);
$Func = Formula("sqrt($A^2 - x^2)");
$Asqr = Compute("$A^2");
#**************
#   Answers
#**************
Context("Interval");
$Domain = Compute("[-$A,$A]");
$Range  = Compute("[0,$A]");
#*******************
#   Display stuff
#*******************
Context("Numeric")->variables->add(y => 'Real');
$Ysqr = Formula("$A^2 - x^2")->reduce;
$Xsqr = Formula("$A^2 - y^2")->reduce;
$XofY = Formula("sqrt($A^2 - y^2)")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML
Find the domain and range of the function [`f(x) = [$Func] `]. Express each in
interval notation.

a) Domain: [_]{$Domain}

b) Range: [_]{$Range}

[@ helpLink('interval') @]*

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For a square root function such as [`f(x) = [$Func] `], the domain is restricted to
all values of [`x`] that do not produce a negative value inside the radical.
Therefore, the domain of this function includes all real values of [`x`] such that 
[`[$Asqr] - x^2 \ge 0`], or [`x^2 \le [$Asqr]`].

This inequality is satisfied for [`-[$A] \le x \le [$A]`].
Expressed in interval form, this is [`[$Domain]`].

By the definition of the square root, the result is strictly nonnegative. Therefore, 
[`f(x) \ge 0`]. To determine if there is an upper bound for the range, set
[`y = [$Func]`] and solve for x:
[``` \begin{aligned}
    y   &=[$Func] \\
    y^2 &=[$Ysqr] \\
    x^2 &=[$Xsqr] \\
    x   &=\pm [$XofY] \\
   \end{aligned}
```]

It is clear that [`-[$A] \le y \le [$A]`]. Since we have already determined that 
[`y \ge 0, 0 \le y \le [$A]`]. Expressed in interval form, this is [`[$Range]`].  

Note that this is the equation for the upper half of a circle with a radius of [$A].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
